---
import Recording from "@/components/Recording.astro"
import CodeBlock from "@/components/CodeBlock.astro"
import Table from "@/components/ui/Table.astro"
import TableBody from "@/components/ui/TableBody.astro"
import TableCell from "@/components/ui/TableCell.astro"
import TableHead from "@/components/ui/TableHead.astro"
import TableHeader from "@/components/ui/TableHeader.astro"
import TableRow from "@/components/ui/TableRow.astro"
import DocsLayout from "@/layouts/DocsLayout.astro"
import { docsMetadata } from "@/lib/docs-meta"

const metadata = docsMetadata("/docs/cli/report")
---
<DocsLayout metadata={metadata}>
    <section>
      <h1 class="mt-0 text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight mb-4">gooze report</h1>
      <p class="gz-prose gz-prose--lead mb-8">A run has finished and you want to see what survived, combine shard results or share
        them. <code>gooze report</code> works with reports Gooze has already written: view them,
        merge sharded results, and store or restore them from an OCI registry.</p>
      <div>
        <div id="usage">
          <h2>Usage</h2>
          <CodeBlock lang="bash" code="gooze report <subcommand> [flags]" />
          <p>All subcommands read from (or write to) the reports directory set by{" "}<code>-o/--output</code>.
            Default is <code>.gooze-reports</code>.</p>
          <p>Subcommands:{" "}<a href="#view"><code>view</code></a>,{" "}<a href="#push"><code>push</code></a>,{" "}<a href="#pull"><code>pull</code></a>, and{" "}<a href="#merge"><code>merge</code></a>.</p>
        </div>
        <div id="view">
          <h2>gooze report view</h2>
          <p>Loads reports from the output directory and prints a per-file score table, one line
            per mutation that counts against the score, and the mutation score.</p>
          <h3>Usage</h3>
          <CodeBlock lang="bash" code="gooze report view [paths...]" />
          <p>If you used a custom output directory, pass it:</p>
          <CodeBlock lang="bash" code="gooze report view -o DIR" />
          <h3>Example output</h3>
          <p>After the first run of the <code>calc</code> example, both bound checks in{" "}<code>calc.go</code> survive:</p>
          <Recording name="calc-report-view" title="Recorded gooze report view after the first run" />
          <p>Each survivor line reads <code>path:line: status (mutagen): original -&gt; mutated</code>.
            Survived, not covered, timed out and skipped mutations are listed; with none, <code>view</code>prints <code>No survivors.</code> The output is the same in a TTY and in CI.</p>
          <div id="view-build-errors">
            <h3>Timeouts and build errors</h3>
            <p>Since <code>v1.4.2</code>, the <code>TIMEOUT</code> and <code>ERROR</code> columns
              make each row add up to <code>MUTATIONS</code>. Mutants that did not build are listed after the survivors, under{" "}<code>Build errors (N):</code>, one line each by file and line, in the same format.
              They still don&apos;t count against the score, and the <code>github</code>,{" "}<code>sarif</code> and <code>codeclimate</code> formats leave them out.</p>
          </div>
          <div id="view-unmerged">
            <h3>Empty main reports</h3>
            <p>Right after a sharded run, or a run with <code>--diff</code> or <code>--run</code>,
              the main reports can be empty, and <code>view</code> prints a score of{" "}<code>100.00%</code> with no survivors. In <code>v1.4.2+</code>, it then adds a line that
              says where the unmerged reports are: the <code>shard_*</code> directories, with the{" "}<code>gooze report merge</code> command to run, or <code>run/</code>, with the{" "}<code>gooze report view -o</code> command that shows it.</p>
          </div>
          <h3>Paths</h3>
          <p>Paths limit the output to matching source files and read like{" "}<a href="/docs/cli/run/#usage"><code>gooze run</code></a> arguments, relative to the module root:</p>
          <ul>
            <li><code>./...</code>: every file (the default)</li>
            <li><code>./pkg/...</code>: files under <code>pkg</code>, recursively</li>
            <li><code>./pkg</code>: files directly in <code>pkg</code></li>
            <li><code>./pkg/file.go</code>: a single file</li>
          </ul>
          <CodeBlock lang="bash" code="gooze report view ./internal/domain/..." />
          <p>If no report matches, <code>view</code> exits <code>6</code> with{" "}<code>no reports in .gooze-reports match ./internal/domain/...</code>. If the reports
            directory does not exist, it names the directory and suggests running{" "}<code>gooze run</code> first or passing <code>-o</code>.</p>
          <div id="view-run">
            <h3>Results of a narrowed run</h3>
            <p>A run with <code>--diff</code>, <code>--run</code> or <code>--mutagens</code>{" "}writes its reports to <code>.gooze-reports/run/</code>, and{" "}<code>view</code> without <code>-o</code> doesn&apos;t read them: it skips
              subdirectories. Point <code>-o</code> at the run to see its score and survivors:</p>
            <CodeBlock lang="bash" code="gooze report view -o .gooze-reports/run" />
            <Recording name="calc-func-run-view" title="Recorded gooze report view -o .gooze-reports/run after a --run run" />
          </div>
          <div id="view-format">
            <h3>Output formats (--format)</h3>
            <p><code>--format</code> chooses how <code>view</code> writes the mutations that count
              against the score. The default comes from <code>report.format</code> in{" "}<code>gooze.yaml</code> (or <code>GOOZE_REPORT_FORMAT</code>).</p>
            <div class="mt-3 rounded border">
              <Table>
                <TableHeader>
                  <TableRow>
                    <TableHead>Format</TableHead>
                    <TableHead>Output</TableHead>
                  </TableRow>
                </TableHeader>
                <TableBody class="text-muted-foreground">
                  <TableRow>
                    <TableCell><code>text</code></TableCell>
                    <TableCell>The summary above (the default).</TableCell>
                  </TableRow>
                  <TableRow>
                    <TableCell><code>github</code></TableCell>
                    <TableCell>A GitHub Actions workflow annotation for each line with survivors, then the
                      summary.</TableCell>
                  </TableRow>
                  <TableRow>
                    <TableCell><code>sarif</code></TableCell>
                    <TableCell>A SARIF 2.1.0 log for code scanning, instead of the summary.</TableCell>
                  </TableRow>
                  <TableRow>
                    <TableCell><code>codeclimate</code>, <code>gitlab</code></TableCell>
                    <TableCell>A Code Climate JSON report for GitLab Code Quality, instead of the summary.
                      Both names write the same report.</TableCell>
                  </TableRow>
                  <TableRow>
                    <TableCell><code>metrics</code></TableCell>
                    <TableCell><code>v1.5.0+</code>. The score and the count of each status as a GitLab
                      metrics report, instead of the summary.</TableCell>
                  </TableRow>
                </TableBody>
              </Table>
            </div>
            <CodeBlock lang="bash" code="gooze report view --format github" />
            <Recording name="calc-report-view-github" title="Recorded gooze report view --format github on calc" />
            <p>Paths in every format but <code>text</code> are relative to the git repository
              root, so they match the files on GitHub and GitLab even when the module is in a
              subdirectory. Outside a git repository, Gooze uses the working directory&apos;s path
              inside <code>$GITHUB_WORKSPACE</code> or, on GitLab, <code>$CI_PROJECT_DIR</code>.</p>
            <p>Thresholds work as with <code>text</code>. With <code>sarif</code>,{" "}<code>codeclimate</code> and <code>metrics</code>, the file is written first, so a gate that fails still
              leaves a complete file:</p>
            <CodeBlock lang="bash" code="gooze report view --format sarif --threshold-total 90 > gooze.sarif" />
            <Recording name="calc-report-view-sarif-gate" title="Recorded gooze report view --format sarif below a threshold" />
            <p>The same with <code>codeclimate</code>, for GitLab Code Quality:</p>
            <CodeBlock lang="bash" code="gooze report view --format codeclimate --threshold-total 90 > gl-code-quality-report.json" />
            <Recording name="calc-report-view-codeclimate" title="Recorded gooze report view --format codeclimate below a threshold" />
            <p><code>--format metrics</code> writes one <code>name value</code> line per metric and
              ends with <code># EOF</code>: <code>gooze_mutation_score_percent</code>, then{" "}<code>gooze_mutants_total</code> and one{" "}<code>gooze_mutants_&lt;status&gt;</code> line for each status. The reports of a{" "}<code>--diff</code> run get <code>gooze_diff_</code> names instead, because their score
              covers only the changed lines and GitLab compares metrics by name with the target
              branch. Without mutations there is no score line, only the counts. See{" "}<a href="/docs/ci/gitlab/#metrics">GitLab CI</a> for the job.</p>
            <p><code>--all</code> always lists mutations as text, so <code>--all</code> with any
              other format exits with code <code>2</code>. An unknown
              format also exits with <code>2</code>. The fields of each format are in{" "}<a href="/docs/reports/#formats">Report format</a>, and the pipelines that use
              them are in <a href="/docs/ci/github/#survivors">GitHub Actions</a> and{" "}<a href="/docs/ci/gitlab/#survivors">GitLab CI</a>.</p>
          </div>
          <div id="view-by-line">
            <h3>One entry per line (--by-line)</h3>
            <p>One weak line often has several surviving mutants, and one test usually kills them
              all. In <code>v1.4.2+</code>, <code>--by-line</code> lists the survivors once per line of a
              file, so the list shows how many places need a test:</p>
            <CodeBlock lang="bash" code="gooze report view --by-line" />
            <ul>
              <li>The heading counts both, as <code>Survivors (N on M lines):</code>.</li>
              <li>A line with several survivors reads{" "}<code>path:line: 5 survived (comparison ×4, numbers): original -&gt; mutated, mutated, ...</code>:
                the count of each status, each mutagen with its count, the original code once, then
                each mutated version without the words it shares with the original at the start and end.</li>
              <li>A line with one survivor reads as it does without the flag.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>It changes only the text summary, also the one <code>--format github</code> prints
              after its annotations. With <code>--all</code>, or with <code>--format sarif</code>,{" "}<code>codeclimate</code> or <code>gitlab</code>, which print no summary, it exits
              with code <code>2</code>. It is off by default, because tools parse the one-line-per-mutant list.</p>
          </div>
          <div id="view-summary">
            <h3>Short output for CI logs (--summary, --top)</h3>
            <p>On a large module the survivor list runs to thousands of lines, too long for a job
              log. In <code>v1.6.0+</code>, <code>--summary</code> prints only the per-file table,
              the score line and the threshold verdict, and <code>--top N</code> keeps the{" "}<code>N</code> files with the most mutants against the score in the table. The{" "}<code>TOTAL FILES</code> row and the score still count every file:</p>
            <CodeBlock lang="bash" code="gooze report view --summary --top 10" />
            <p>Under GitLab CI (<code>GITLAB_CI=true</code>), the table, the survivors and the build
              errors are <a href="https://docs.gitlab.com/ci/jobs/job_logs/#custom-collapsible-sections">collapsible sections</a> of the job log; the
              survivors and build errors start collapsed. <code>--summary</code> can&apos;t be combined with{" "}<code>--all</code>, <code>--by-line</code> or a <code>--format</code> that prints no
              summary (exit code <code>2</code>).</p>
          </div>
          <div id="view-write">
            <h3>Several reports at once (--write)</h3>
            <p>In <code>v1.6.0+</code>, <code>--write &lt;format&gt;=&lt;path&gt;</code> writes a report
              file besides the output, and repeats for each file you want. Gooze loads and scores the
              reports once for all of them:</p>
            <CodeBlock lang="bash" code="gooze report view --summary --write gitlab=gl-code-quality-report.json --write metrics=metrics.txt --write text=report.txt" />
            <ul>
              <li>The formats are <code>text</code>, <code>gitlab</code>, <code>codeclimate</code>,{" "}<code>sarif</code> and <code>metrics</code>. <code>github</code> annotations only work in
                the job log, so they stay on <code>--format</code>.</li>
              <li>A <code>text</code> file holds the whole report: <code>--summary</code>,{" "}<code>--top</code> and the GitLab sections shape only the output.</li>
              <li>stdout keeps <code>--format</code>, and the files never change the exit code.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div id="view-gate">
            <h3>Which score fails (--gate)</h3>
            <p>In <code>v1.6.0+</code>, <code>--gate</code> (or <code>report.gate</code> in{" "}<code>gooze.yaml</code>) picks the scope whose thresholds decide exit code{" "}<code>3</code>: <code>diff</code> for the changed lines of a <code>--diff</code> run,{" "}<code>total</code> for the whole project, or <code>none</code>. The default,{" "}<code>auto</code>, gates on the reports&apos; own scope, as before. A scope the gate leaves
              out is shown but never fails. A <code>--diff</code> run&apos;s score line names the changed
              lines.</p>
          </div>
          <h3>Every mutation (--all)</h3>
          <p><code>--all</code> lists every mutation with its status and diff instead of the
            summary. It takes the same paths:</p>
          <CodeBlock lang="bash" code="gooze report view --all ./cmd/run.go" />
          <p>In a TTY, <code>--all</code> opens an interactive list where you can:</p>
          <ul>
            <li>See the mutation score</li>
            <li>Scroll results by file/type/status</li>
            <li>Filter by id/file/type/status</li>
            <li>Open a diff for any mutation that was not killed (<code>survived</code>,{" "}<code>not_covered</code>, <code>timeout</code>, <code>error</code>)</li>
          </ul>
          <h3>Keybindings (TTY, --all)</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><code>↑/k</code>, <code>↓/j</code>: move</li>
            <li><code>g</code> / <code>G</code>: top / bottom</li>
            <li><code>/</code>: filter</li>
            <li><code>enter</code> / <code>space</code>: toggle diff</li>
            <li><code>q</code>: quit</li>
          </ul>
          <p>When not interactive (for example in CI or piped), <code>--all</code> prints every
            mutation linearly, with diffs for the ones not killed:</p>
          <CodeBlock lang="bash" code="gooze report view --all | cat" />
          <h3>Thresholds</h3>
          <p><code>--threshold-total</code>, <code>--threshold-average</code> and{" "}<code>--threshold-file</code> (or <code>threshold.*</code> in{" "}<code>gooze.yaml</code>) make <code>view</code> exit with code <code>3</code> when
            a score of the viewed reports is below them, so a last CI step can gate on the
            reports. <code>--threshold-max-survivors</code> does the same when more mutants
            than it allows survive. Paths limit which files count. See{" "}<a href="/docs/ci/quality-gates/">Quality gates</a>.</p>
        </div>
        <div id="push">
          <h2>gooze report push</h2>
          <p>Packages the reports directory and pushes it to an OCI registry as an artifact.
            Use this to share baseline results with CI and teammates.</p>
          <h3>Usage</h3>
          <CodeBlock lang="bash" code="gooze report push <reference>" />
          <p>The reference must include a tag (digest references are rejected), and the reports directory
            must exist and contain files. Symlinks inside it are skipped.</p>
          <h3>Example</h3>
          <CodeBlock lang="bash" code="gooze report push ghcr.io/your-org/your-repo/gooze-reports:main" />
          <h3>Flags</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><code>-o, --output &lt;dir&gt;</code>: reports directory to package (default <code>.gooze-reports</code>)</li>
            <li><code>--plain-http</code>: use a non-TLS (HTTP) registry</li>
            <li><code>--insecure</code>: skip TLS certificate verification</li>
            <li><code>--oci-compat</code>: push with only widely accepted media types, for registries such as GitLab&apos;s that reject others (config <code>report.oci.compat</code>; on by itself for{" "}<code>registry.gitlab.com</code> and the hosts in <code>report.oci.compat_hosts</code>; see{" "}<a href="/docs/ci/gitlab/#registry-media-types">GitLab registry</a>). <code>v1.5.0+</code></li>
          </ul>
          <div id="auth">
            <h3>Authentication</h3>
            <p>For registries that require login, set{" "}<code>GOOZE_REGISTRY_USERNAME</code> and{" "}<code>GOOZE_REGISTRY_PASSWORD</code>. The
              password may be a token or PAT. For GHCR, use your GitHub username with a PAT (or{" "}<code>GITHUB_TOKEN</code> in CI). The same
              variables apply to <a href="#pull"><code>pull</code></a>.</p>
            <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`export GOOZE_REGISTRY_USERNAME="$USER"
export GOOZE_REGISTRY_PASSWORD="$GITHUB_TOKEN"
gooze report push ghcr.io/your-org/your-repo/gooze-reports:main`} />
            <p>If both are unset, Gooze falls back to the Docker credential store (whatever{" "}<code>docker login</code> saved). Setting{" "}<code>GOOZE_REGISTRY_USERNAME</code> without{" "}<code>GOOZE_REGISTRY_PASSWORD</code> is an error;{" "}<code>GOOZE_REGISTRY_PASSWORD</code> alone is sent as a bearer token. See{" "}<a href="/docs/ci/sharing-reports/">Sharing reports (OCI)</a>{" "}for the full baseline workflow.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div id="pull">
          <h2>gooze report pull</h2>
          <p>Pulls reports from an OCI registry and extracts them into the reports directory.
            Use this to restore a baseline before an incremental run.</p>
          <p>In <code>v1.6.0+</code>, a pull replaces the stored reports instead of adding to them,
            so a report of a file that the baseline no longer has doesn&apos;t stay behind. It keeps
            the <code>shard_*</code> and <code>run</code> directories, which wait for{" "}<a href="#merge"><code>merge</code></a>, and changes nothing when the pull fails. It prints the
            reference, the digest, the number of report files and the pulled score.</p>
          <h3>Usage</h3>
          <CodeBlock lang="bash" code="gooze report pull <reference>" />
          <p>The reference must include a tag or a digest. Extracting into the current directory
            (<code>-o .</code>) works, file modes are preserved, and pulling an artifact that was not
            pushed by Gooze fails with an error saying it is not a gooze reports artifact. Pull reads
            artifacts pushed with or without <code>--oci-compat</code>.</p>
          <h3>Example</h3>
          <CodeBlock lang="bash" code="gooze report pull ghcr.io/your-org/your-repo/gooze-reports:main" />
          <h3>Flags</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><code>-o, --output &lt;dir&gt;</code>: reports directory to extract into (default <code>.gooze-reports</code>)</li>
            <li><code>--plain-http</code>: use a non-TLS (HTTP) registry</li>
            <li><code>--insecure</code>: skip TLS certificate verification</li>
          </ul>
          <h3>Authentication</h3>
          <p>Pull uses the same credentials as push: see{" "}<a href="#auth">Authentication</a> above.</p>
        </div>
        <div id="merge">
          <h2>gooze report merge</h2>
          <p>Merges sharded reports (from <code>shard_*</code> folders) into the base output
            directory. It also folds in the reports of a narrowed run
            (<code>run/</code> and <code>run/shard_*</code>) and removes <code>run/</code>.</p>
          <h3>Usage</h3>
          <CodeBlock lang="bash" code="gooze report merge" />
          <p>In <code>v1.6.0+</code>, <code>merge</code> shows the merged reports as{" "}<code>view</code> does, with the same <code>--format</code>, <code>--summary</code>,{" "}<code>--top</code>, <code>--by-line</code>, <code>--write</code> and <code>--gate</code>{" "}flags. Earlier versions printed the run&apos;s progress view and the score.</p>
          <p>It takes the same threshold flags as <code>view</code>, checked on the merged
            reports. A shard doesn&apos;t check thresholds, so put them here when you shard. For the
            shards of a <code>--diff</code> run (<code>gooze report merge -o .gooze-reports/run</code>),
            the diff minimums apply (<code>--threshold-diff-*</code>, <code>threshold.diff.*</code>), as
            for <code>view</code> (<code>v1.4.2+</code>). Earlier versions checked the plain minimums there.</p>
          <div id="merge-scopes">
            <h3>Both scopes of a merge request (--scope)</h3>
            <p>A merge request has two scores: its changed lines and the whole project. In{" "}<code>v1.6.0+</code>, one command reports both:</p>
            <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`gooze report merge --scope diff,total --summary \\
  --write-diff gitlab=gl-code-quality-report.json \\
  --write metrics=metrics.txt --write text=report.txt`} />
            <ol>
              <li>It merges the <code>--diff</code> shards in <code>.gooze-reports/run</code> and shows the
                changed lines. <code>--write-diff</code> writes their files.</li>
              <li>It folds them into the baseline in <code>.gooze-reports</code> and shows the whole
                project. <code>--write</code> writes its files, and <code>--format</code> applies here.</li>
              <li>The merged <code>.gooze-reports/run</code> stays, so{" "}<code>gooze report view -o .gooze-reports/run</code> still shows the changed lines.</li>
            </ol>
            <p>The gate defaults to the changed lines, so the whole project&apos;s score is shown but
              doesn&apos;t fail the job; <code>--gate total</code> or <code>none</code> changes that. The{" "}<code>metrics</code> file holds both scopes,{" "}<code>gooze_*</code> and <code>gooze_diff_*</code>, with one <code>#&nbsp;EOF</code>. Without a{" "}<code>--diff</code> run in <code>run/</code>, <code>--scope diff,total</code> exits with code{" "}<code>6</code>.</p>
          </div>
          <h3>When you need this</h3>
          <p>If you run <code>gooze run -s INDEX/TOTAL</code>, Gooze writes into shard folders.
            You merge them before running <code>gooze report view</code>. After a run with{" "}<code>--diff</code>, <code>--run</code> or <code>--mutagens</code>, merge to add its
            results to the main reports before you read the project score.</p>
          <h3>Example</h3>
          <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`gooze run -s 0/4 ./...
gooze run -s 1/4 ./...
gooze run -s 2/4 ./...
gooze run -s 3/4 ./...

gooze report merge
gooze report view`} />
          <p>This example splits work into 4 jobs, then merges, then prints the full score and survivors.</p>
          <h3>Notes</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>If there are no shard directories, <code>merge</code> just rebuilds the index and shows the reports.</li>
            <li>Shard reports replace base reports for the same source file; base reports for files
              no shard re-tested are kept, so caching can still work.</li>
            <li>Reports in <code>run/</code> (and <code>run/shard_*</code>) are folded in after the
              shards. For a file unchanged since the main reports (same source and test file
              hashes), they replace only the matching mutations, and the file stays complete. For
              a changed or new file, they replace all of the file&apos;s results; if the run tested
              only part of the file, it is marked partial, and the next full{" "}<code>gooze run</code> re-tests it in full. In <code>v1.4.2+</code>, a{" "}<code>--mutagens</code> run alone doesn&apos;t make a file partial: if the file hasn&apos;t changed since, the
              next full run tests only the mutants of the other mutagens. Then <code>run/</code> is removed, unless{" "}<code>--scope diff,total</code> keeps it.</li>
            <li>When <code>merge</code> runs inside the checkout, base reports for files that no longer exist are dropped.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
</DocsLayout>
